//? Title: Monotone cubic interpolation
//? Subtitle: Prepared Hermite curves, actual slope limiting and endpoint contracts
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Interpolate ordered observations with a smooth curve without introducing
reversals or overshoot between neighboring values. We derive slope-limited cubic
Hermite pieces, inspect actual shared-tangent changes, retain the original step
comparison with a natural cubic spline, and demonstrate finite-query endpoint
clamping. A prepared native model avoids rebuilding slopes for every plotted
point.
)

\toc

> Cubic pieces and the limiter

For interval width h=x[i+1]−x[i], normalized position t=(x−x[i])/h and endpoint
tangents m[i],m[i+1], use the cubic Hermite basis. It reproduces the endpoint
ordinates and tangents, giving a globally C1 interpolant when adjacent pieces
share tangent values.

$$
p(t)=(2t^3-3t^2+1)y_i+(t^3-2t^2+t)hm_i+(-2t^3+3t^2)y_{i+1}+(t^3-t^2)hm_{i+1}.
$$
\alttext(The cubic Hermite basis combines two endpoint values and two endpoint slopes.)

Let delta=(y[i+1]−y[i])/h. For nonzero delta, normalize the endpoint slopes as
alpha=m[i]/delta and beta=m[i+1]/delta. A sufficient monotonicity region is
alpha,beta nonnegative with alpha squared plus beta squared at most9. The
derivative divided by delta is the quadratic6t(1−t)+alpha(1−4t+3t
squared)+beta(3t squared−2t), which is nonnegative throughout this region. Hence
each interval remains between its endpoint values in exact arithmetic. This is a
sufficient region, not a necessary characterization of every monotone cubic.

The producer begins with endpoint secants and interior averages. In
left-to-right order it zeroes both tangents on a flat interval, clamps
wrong-sign tangents, then radially scales a remaining pair outside the radius3
region. Later intervals only decrease a shared tangent's magnitude, preserving
earlier sufficient bounds. Final tangents define evaluation; a decision record
describes the state at that time and can precede later changes to its shared
right tangent.

> Three nodes: a plateau followed by a rise

Nodes(0,0),(1,0),(2,1) initially have tangents0,0.5,1. The flat first interval
zeroes its two tangents, leaving final tangents0,0,1. The second interval
therefore has p(t)=2t squared−t cubed and value0.375 at its midpoint. The
diagram reads the actual first limiting decision.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/monotone_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::algorithms::MonotoneCubic c({0, 1, 2}, {0, 0, 1});
    auto &r = c.decisions()[0];
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("a", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual interval {}\nsecant {}", r.interval, r.secant)).node("b", {6, 0}, std::format("Actual tangents before\n{} / {}", r.left_before, r.right_before)).node("c", {12, 0}, std::format("Actual flat limit after\n{} / {}", r.left_after, r.right_after)).edge("a", "b").edge("b", "c");
    std::print("| node | value | final tangent |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < c.xs().size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", c.xs()[i], c.ys()[i], c.tangents()[i]);
    std::print("\nMidpoint value: {}.\n", c.evaluate(1.5));
    d.save_svg("build/monotone_tiny.svg");
}
```
